.TH PEEK 1 "2026-09-07" "peek" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
peek \- preview files without opening them
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B peek
.RI [ FILE .\|.\|.]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B peek
is a QuickLook-class file previewer for the COSMIC desktop. Pointed at a file,
it shows the file \(em the picture, the pages, the video, the source with its
syntax coloured, the contents of the archive \(em without opening an
application.
.PP
The first invocation becomes a resident daemon and stays running; later
invocations reach it over D-Bus. That is deliberate: creating a wgpu device,
deserialising the syntax set, and building GStreamer's registry all happen once
at startup rather than once per keypress, and the daemon is what keeps the
previewer service on the bus for a file manager to find.
.PP
With no arguments,
.B peek
toggles the last preview, which is what makes the command itself the right
thing to bind to a keyboard shortcut.
.SH OPTIONS
.B peek
takes file paths and no flags. Everything else is a setting; see
.B CONFIGURATION .
.SH KEYS
.TP
.B Space ", " Esc
Back: leave the index sheet or fullscreen if either is showing, and close the
preview otherwise.
.TP
.B Left ", " Right
Previous and next file, or the previous and next cell in the index sheet.
.TP
.B Up ", " Down
Previous and next page \(em or file, where there are no pages, or a row in the
index sheet.
.TP
.B Shift+Left ", " Shift+Right
Seek five seconds through a media file.
.TP
.B P
Play and pause.
.TP
.BR + ", " - ", " 0
Zoom in, out, and back to the natural fit.
.TP
.B F ", " F11
Fullscreen: the chrome yields and the file gets the display.
.TP
.B G
The index sheet: every file in the selection, at once.
.TP
.B Ctrl+C
Copy the file's path.
.TP
.B Enter
Open in the default application, or open the cell the index sheet's cursor is
on.
.PP
The pointer works too: scrolling over an image, page, or video zooms it,
dragging pans it once zoom has cropped it, and double-clicking jumps between
the fitted view and the file's own pixels.
.SH CONFIGURATION
Settings are reachable from the gear in the overlay's header, and are stored
through
.BR cosmic-config ,
under
.IR ~/.config/cosmic/dev.entro314labs.Peek/v1/ ,
one RON file per key. Changes are applied live.
.PP
The keys are
.BR blur ,
.BR opacity ,
.BR autoplay ,
.BR loop_media ,
.BR line_numbers ,
.BR max_fraction ,
.BR nautilus_previewer ,
.BR follow_selection ,
.BR click_away ,
and
.BR animate .
An unset key uses its default; an unreadable one is logged and falls back, so a
bad value cannot stop a preview opening.
.SH PLUGINS
File types
.B peek
does not handle can be added with a TOML file in
.IR ~/.local/share/peek/plugins/ ,
either by routing the type to a previewer that already exists or by naming a
command that renders it. Plugins are consulted only for files that would
otherwise show the metadata card, so installing one cannot change or break an
existing preview. See the
.I README
in
.I /usr/share/peek/plugins/
for the format.
.SH THE SPACE BAR
.B peek
serves
.BR org.gnome.NautilusPreviewer2 ,
which is the interface a file manager calls when the space bar is pressed, so
in Nautilus the gesture works with no further setup. GNOME's own previewer,
.BR sushi ,
claims the same bus name;
.B peek
asks to replace it and hands it back on exit. To make the takeover survive a
reboot:
.PP
.RS
.B systemctl --user mask --now org.gnome.NautilusPreviewer.service
.RE
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ~/.config/cosmic/dev.entro314labs.Peek/v1/
Settings, one file per key.
.TP
.I ~/.local/share/peek/plugins/
Plugin manifests.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B RUST_LOG
Log filter, e.g.
.BR peek=debug .
Logs go to the journal \(em
.B journalctl --user -t peek -f
\(em and to stderr only when there is no journal to write to, because a daemon
started by D-Bus activation has a stderr nobody can see.
.TP
.B PEEK_PLUGIN_DIR
Overrides the plugin search path entirely. For testing a plugin without
installing it.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR peek-thumbnailer (1)
